<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Commission on the Truth for El Salvador</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1992">1992</a>–<a href="/years/1993">1993</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Salvadoran Truth Commission</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Commission on the Truth for El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> body created under the peace accords of 1992 to investigate serious acts of violence during the civil war. Its report of March 1993, From Madness to Hope, attributed the great majority of the cases it examined to the armed forces and allied death squads. Days later the legislature passed a broad amnesty law.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/commission-on-the-truth-for-el-salvador.jpg" alt="Commission on the Truth for El Salvador" width="320" height="162" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CIA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:El_Salvador-CIA_WFB_Map_(2004).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The civil war between the Salvadoran government and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farabundo-marti-national-liberation-front">Farabundo Martí National Liberation Front</a> (FMLN), a union of five leftist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> groups formed in October 1980, followed the coup of October 1979 and the assassination of Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscar-romero">Óscar Romero</a> on 24 March 1980. By its end in 1992 an estimated 75,000 Salvadorans had been killed, about 1.4 percent of the population. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> gave the government approximately $4.5 billion in military and other aid during the 1980s.</p><p>Talks mediated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> began in July 1990. The Mexico Agreement of 1991 called for a truth commission, and the Act of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> of December 1991 brought the agreements together before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chapultepec-peace-accords">Chapultepec Peace Accords</a> of 16 January 1992. With the consent of both sides, Secretary-General Javier Pérez de Cuéllar appointed three foreigners: Belisario Betancur, former president of Colombia, Thomas Buergenthal, former president of the Inter-American Court of Human Rights, and Reinaldo Figueredo Planchart, former foreign minister of Venezuela.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The commission worked from July 1992 to March 1993 with offices in Chalatenango, Santa Ana and San Miguel and a staff with no Salvadorans. Its mandate covered serious acts of violence between January 1980 and July 1991 whose impact demanded that the public know the truth. It heard about 2,000 witnesses and gathered statements from more than 20,000 others through human rights groups. In November 1992 the Argentine Forensic Anthropology Team exhumed remains of at least 143 victims at El Mozote, supporting testimony that they were summarily executed.</p><p>The report attributed approximately 85 percent of the abuses to government forces and named more than 40 senior members of the military, the judiciary and the armed opposition. It recommended removing those responsible from public office, reforming the judicial system, paying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> to victims and creating a forum to oversee all this.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The army's high command called the report unfair, biased and insolent, and President Alfredo Cristiani said Salvadorans wanted to forget the past; others criticised it for passing over the role of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. About 200 senior officers were removed, retiring with full honours, and a new Criminal Procedure Code followed in 1996. In 2016 the Supreme Court ruled the amnesty law of 20 March 1993 unconstitutional, opening the way to prosecutions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Truth Commission for El Salvador” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Chapultepec Peace Accords of 1992, negotiated under United Nations auspices, became a model for UN-brokered settlements, creating a new civilian police and a <b>Truth Commission</b> whose report of 1993 attributed the great majority of abuses to the armed forces and allied death squads.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">Republic of El Salvador · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
